Business Insights. Subscription Churn & Revenue Cohort Analysis

A SQL-driven analysis of a subscription-based platforms user, subscription and payment data. We ran the analysis to uncover why customers churn and where revenue is concentrated than simply counting churn.
Dataset: 120 users · 190 subscriptions · 2,180 payment transactions Tools: MySQL (joins, CASE, GROUP BY, window functions, CTEs)

 Key Findings
1. Overall churn is high. 60%
Out of 190 subscriptions in the dataset 114 have churned and only 76 remain active. This is above the 20–30% annual churn that is generally considered healthy for subscription businesses indicating a retention problem rather than an acquisition problem.
2. Acquisition channel quality varies by up to 23 points
Channel Churn Rate
Email Campaign 43.5% (best)
Affiliate 57.1%
Referral 60.9%
Social Media 61.5%
Organic Search 63.0%
Paid Search 63.9%
Direct 66.7% (worst)

Users acquired through email campaigns retain better than any other channel. Direct signups. The lowest-intent least-nurtured acquisition path. Churn the most.
Business implication: acquisition spend and lifecycle nurturing should be reweighted toward channels that bring in higher-retention users, not higher volume.

3. Mid-tier plans retain longer than premium tiers
Plan Avg. Days Before Churn
Standard 141.8
Basic 111.7
Enterprise 107.8
Pro 99.6
Premium 98.5
Standard-plan subscribers stay over 40 days longer on average than Premium subscribers. This is a counter-intuitive SaaS pattern. Higher-tier customers often churn faster if onboarding does not quickly prove the extra value they are paying for.
Business implication: the Premium/Pro onboarding experience deserves a look. Higher price should not correlate with lower loyalty.

4. Revenue is concentrated not spread
The top 10 customers by lifetime spend contributed between $2,150 and $4,270 each. The single highest-value customer alone is more than 4% of total platform revenue.
Mexico and Spain together generate one-third of total revenue despite representing under 20% of the user base.
Business implication: losing a handful of top accounts or a single high-revenue market can have an outsized impact. High-LTV customers should be proactively. Flagged for retention outreach before they show cancellation signals. Not treated the same as an average user.

5. Revenue has grown consistently over time
Monthly revenue grew from about $39 in January 2023 to a peak above $3,790 in 2026 showing steady platform growth despite the high churn rate. This means user and payment volume is currently outpacing churn but this is a fragile position if churn is not addressed.

Recommendations

Shift acquisition spend toward email-driven campaigns. They retain 23 points better than Direct traffic.
Audit the Premium/Pro onboarding flow. High price is not translating into retention, which suggests a value-realization gap early in the customer journey.
Build a high-LTV retention program. Proactively monitor spending customers and revenue-concentrated markets (Mexico, Spain) rather than applying a one‑size‑fits‑all retention strategy.
Segment retention efforts by channel and plan not the user base, as a whole. Churn drivers clearly differ by acquisition source and pricing tier so a targeted fix will outperform an one.
